<Article
  title="Water from the air, bottled in the room"
  description="Return to Aura. One beautiful room at a time."
  kicker="Water"
  date="2026-09-30"
>
  <p>An atmospheric water generator condenses moisture from the air. On this room it is a low unit on the roof, not a municipal plant. Inside, a short line fills plastic jugs.</p>
  <RevealHeading as="h2">What it is for</RevealHeading>
  <p>A site with humidity and power can make drinking water without a well. Thirst Contact is that idea on this room, and it comes later. The generator and the line still need further research and development. Homes and the farm units are what you can ask about now. The amount of water, when the machine exists, is a function of humidity, temperature, and electricity. Dry winter air makes less. Any page that quotes litres per day without the weather is guessing, so this one does not.</p>
  <RevealHeading as="h2">The jugs</RevealHeading>
  <p>The jugs are plastic, because that is the container a later line is designed around: light, familiar, and able to carry a label. A label can be blank when you are bottling for yourself. An advertiser’s label is a later idea, described in <a href="/journal/free-food-and-water">the network note</a>. Water that leaves the room still has to meet the rules of the place it is filled. The machine does not replace that.</p>
  <RevealHeading as="h2">The room around the line</RevealHeading>
  <p>The line is meant to leave the room livable. It is not a factory floor. Glass, oak, and the meadow stay. See the arrangement on <a href="/water">the water page</a>, and the room it belongs to, <a href="/#shell">on your land</a>.</p>
</Article>
